le figure it out soon I think  this is probably an area of research for  a lot of people and there's a lot of  interest right now and how what the  bacterial responses to the anti CRISPR  development and how the war has  continued because it's always  ping-ponging back and forth once one  side develops some sort of defense the  other side finds a way to overcome it so  I think that war is continuing and maybe  the anti anti crispers will also be  really important biotechnology tools it  remains to be seen  and we'll wrap up with one more question  and that question is what is the status  of the various potential clinical uses  of CRISPR genome editing so where do we  stand on clinical uses of CRISPR there's  tons and tons of interest lots of  different labs are working on various  ways to use CRISPR to combat disease and  people so far the only clinical trials  that have been initiated are to look at  cancer therapies so actually it's kind  of a timely moment so yesterday the FDA  just approved the first car T cell  therapy to combat cancer from Novartis  and that's a technique that people are  trying to incorporate CRISPR into so you  have to engineer basically how that  works is you take t-cells out of people  someone with cancer and then engineer  them to be better at killing tumors and  you could use CRISPR for the engineering  step and then you put the cells back  into the person and hope that they'll  they'll be able to find the tumors and  destroy them and so yeah people are  trying to use CRISPR for this not quite  I don't think that one of the trials  maybe use cart T cells but a lot of them  are a kind of slightly different  approach but still engineering T cells  for cancer I think there might be one or  two additional newer trials coming out  sort of similar uses but a lot of the  efforts are sort of right at the  precipice of getting started so there's  a lot of people doing preclinical work  that are going to start clinical trials  in the next one to three years or so and  I think we can expect to see some of  those starting pretty soon I think yes  so far there's nothing really up and  running for treating genetic disease so  that's the main thing that people are  excited about it I think for the most  part with crispers that you could  potentially correct underlying defects  in people's DNA that is causing disease  for that  and do it permanently so it could  potentially be a cure if you can  actually get these gene editing reagents  to the part of the body they need to be  in and successfully tweak the DNA you  could completely stop whatever the  disease is permanently because your DNA  shouldn't be changing otherwise so  applications like that could be used for  these diseases that we've understood for  a really really long time but not been  able to treat at all beyond treating the  symptoms so that would include things  like sickle cell disease and cystic  fibrosis Huntington's disease tay-sachs  all kinds of things that have these  really well understood mutations that  lead to the pathology they all have  potential they're all potentially  treated using CRISPR but we're kind of  almost there not quite there there's  still a few things that need to be  overcome which is a kind of longer  question but I think where you can  expect some really interesting stuff to  come out in the next couple years  I would like to once again thank dr.  Hawk Strasser for her presentation do  you have any final comments I would just  say thanks for listening if you have  more questions I can probably respond to  them over time dad I hope you enjoyed  the presentation and stay tuned for all  the CRISPR research that's yet to come  thank you once again dr. Hawke Strasser  I would also like to thank lab roots for  making today's educational webcast  possible before we go I want to let  everyone know that today's webcast will  be available for on-demand viewing  through December 14 2017 you will  receive an email from LaBrie letting you  know when this webcast will be available  for replay please share that  announcement with your colleagues who  may have missed today's live event  that's all for now thank you for joining  us we hope to see you again soon goodbye
 

